(in comparison with the total population. For example, approximately) Tj T*
(45,000 cases were reported for the entire United States and Canada in) Tj T*
(1949??and this was a bad year.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Furthermore, about 50% of those who get polio seriously enough to) Tj T*
(become paralyzed recover completely. Another large group has very) Tj T*
(slight after-effects. Comparatively few are seriously affected by the) Tj T*
(disease, and many of these can be considerably improved by means of) Tj T*
(surgery and prolonged special treatment. Very few cases end fatally.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This does not mean, however, that polio can safely be ignored. The) Tj T*
(disease is a serious one??it is the most frequent cause of deformity in) Tj T*
(children??and wise parents will take every possible precaution to help) Tj T*
(their children avoid contracting it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(                           _What is_ POLIO?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is a contagious disease in which the gray matter of the spinal) Tj T*
(cord is attacked, producing destruction of certain cells and causing) Tj T*
(paralysis. The disease is caused by a virus??tiny ?bugs? smaller than) Tj T*
(bacteria. Apparently these ?bugs? enter through the mouth on food or in) Tj T*
(water that has been contaminated in some fashion, or through the nose) Tj T*
(in breathing. It is not conclusively known how the disease is spread.) Tj T*
(Many people harbor the virus without any reaction whatsoever. Many) Tj T*
(others have mild symptoms which they disregard as a summer cold. Once) Tj T*
(symptoms have developed, special laboratory studies made by experts are) Tj T*
(usually carried out to establish the diagnosis accurately.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(                       _What are the_ SYMPTOMS?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There is no characteristic symptom or sign to be found in all cases.) Tj T*
(This makes the diagnosis difficult. Quite frequently, the patient) Tj T*
(cannot bend his head forward as far as usual. A doctor should) Tj T*
(always be called if anyone in the family has a fever associated with a) Tj T*
(bad headache, or with pain in the muscles in either the arms or legs or) Tj T*
(in the neck and back. Even minor upsets in children should be looked) Tj T*
(upon with suspicion. Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, a slight cold coupled) Tj T*
(with pain in the joints, are also possible danger signs. A child) Tj T*
(catching polio may become drowsy or restless, feverish and irritable.) Tj T*
(He may complain about and resist being moved. These symptoms are) Tj T*
(particularly important during the summer and early fall months because) Tj T*
(this is the period during which most outbreaks of polio occur.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(                       _What is the_ TREATMENT?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If, during the course of an epidemic, any suspicious illness should) Tj T*
(develop in a member of your family, prompt medical attention and) Tj T*
ET
